      "budget_share": 0.7,
      "why": "unrolled_k is the only family in my assignment that can plausibly meet the throughput constraint. The spec requires throughput_msps >= 32, which means the unit must produce at least 32 results per microsecond. For unrolled_k, throughput = Fmax * k (since it produces k results per cycle when fully unrolled, or more precisely, one result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so throughput = Fmax / (ceil(N/k)+3) * ... wait, let me reconsider. Actually, unrolled_k produces one result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles. So throughput_msps = Fmax_MHz / (ceil(N/k)+3). To get >= 32 Msps, we need Fmax / (ceil(N/k)+3) >= 32. If Fmax is around 100-200 MHz, then ceil(N/k)+3 must be <= 3-6. With k=4..8 and N=12..16, ceil(N/k) is 2-4, so total cycles is 5-7. At 100 MHz, that gives 14-20 Msps, which is below 32. At 200 MHz, that gives 28-40 Msps. So we need high Fmax and high k. k=8 with N=12 gives ceil(12/8)=2, so 5 cycles. At 160 MHz, that's 32 Msps. This is tight but feasible. The area will be larger due to the unrolled datapath, but it's the only way to meet throughput in my assigned families. I'll focus on higher k values and moderate N to balance area and throughput."
